  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${b}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+1 & ={(4{x}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (4{x}^{2})\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{2}+1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+1 & =4{x}^{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-({i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(2x)}^{2}-{(i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(2x+i)(2x-i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+1 & ={(4{x}^{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((2x+i)(2x-i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(2x+i)}^{2}{(2x-i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
